# 14 Op flags: unbounded, unguarded, reproducible
#
# Shows the three acknowledgments an op can carry between its parameters
# and the `=`. Each one is a greppable word that a reviewer sees in the
# deploy diff.
#   - `unbounded`: the op's work grows with the size of the tenant. An op
#     with no provable row bound fails registration without the word. The
#     bounds the compiler accepts: a unique address, `take`, a bounded
#     parameter, a `reduce`, and a keyset page over a total order (ruling
#     2026-09-05). A plain listing is paged, so it is bounded and the word
#     is optional on it. A many-side join breaks the page's total order, so
#     it needs the word. A projection that a `group` or `reduce` folds away
#     opens payloads the page never sees, so sealed work there needs it too.
#     The files here write the word on every op that scans, so a reader sees
#     the scan.
#   - `unguarded`: bypass the row guards for the entity sources written in
#     THIS op's body. Used for admin and report ops. It does not strip a
#     callee's own guards.
#   - `reproducible`: the same call gives the same answer later. It forbids
#     the clock: a `since` window anywhere in the op's own pipeline, or a
#     `filled` parameter. Either answers `reproducible_clock`. Bound the
#     window with a date parameter instead, as `closedBefore` does.
#   - An op that composes an unguarded callee must repeat `unguarded`.
#     An op that composes an unbounded callee must repeat `unbounded`.
#   - Marking `unguarded` on an op that touches no guarded entity is an error.
#
# Reference: docs/definition-language.md section 3 G14, section 5 "Cost
# classes", section 13 "Open items" (op wrapper).

domain projects @ 1.0.0

entity projects {
  name:  text
  owner  -> members
  access read   owner == $caller
  access write  owner == $caller
}

entity tasks {
  project -> projects owned
  title:  text
  status: text = "open"
  closed: date?
}

# Bounded by `take`, so it needs no flag.
op firstFive =
  tasks[title]
  sort title
  take 5

# Scans every row the caller may see, so it says so.
op allMine unbounded =
  tasks[title, status]

# An admin report over every project, whoever owns it. Both words are loud.
op allTasksEverywhere unbounded unguarded =
  tasks[title, status, project.name]
  sort project.name, title

op openCountsPerProject unbounded unguarded =
  tasks(status == "open")[project.name]
  group project.name { open: count }
  sort -open

# Composing an unguarded op means saying so again.
op worstProject unbounded unguarded =
  openCountsPerProject[name, open]
  sort -open
  take 1

# `reproducible` forbids `since` and `filled`. This one cannot drift with the
# clock: `$day` is supplied, never filled.
op closedBefore($day: date) unbounded reproducible =
  tasks(closed < $day)[title, closed]
  sort closed
